    <text>
        <body>
            <div type="edition" subtype="primary" xml:space="preserve" xml:lang="grc" resp="#VM">
                <ab>
                    <lb n="1"/><persName type="attested"><name>Παντάρε̄ς</name></persName> μ’ ἀνέθε̄κ<supplied reason="lost">ε</supplied> <persName type="attested"><name>Μενεκράτιος</name></persName> Διὸ<supplied reason="lost" cert="low">ς ἆθλον</supplied> <unclear>τ</unclear>ο͂ <placeName type="ethnic">Γελοαίο̄</placeName>
                </ab>
            </div>
            <div type="apparatus">
                <listApp>
                    <app>
                        <note>Text after Jeffery 1961 (LSAG, p. 273 and no. 48), as reproduced by Arena 2002 (IGASMG II² 4).</note>
                    </app>
                    <app loc="1">
                        <note>Jeffery's restoration Διὸ[ς ἆθλον] το͂ Γελοαίο̄ is adopted here. Ebert 1972 restores two hexameters, Διὸ[ς ἆθλα / hίπ(π)οι νικάσας πέδο ἐ(κ)κλε]το͂ Γελοαίο̄, interpreting the dedication as the prize 
                            for a victory in the horse race. IvO 142 and earlier editions instead reconstruct a victory in the chariot race. Since the original extent of the plinth is unknown, all extended restorations remain conjectural.</note>
                    </app>
                </listApp>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="en" resp="#VM">
                <p>Pantares, son of Menekrates the Geloan, dedicated me as a prize of Zeus.</p>
                <p>Pantares,  the  son  of  Menekrates,  from  the  renowned  plain  of  Gela,  dedicated  me,  after  having  won by horse the contests of Zeus. [Alternative translation by Fouquet following Ebert's edition]</p>
            </div>
            <div type="translation" xml:lang="it" resp="#VM">
                <p>Pantares, figlio di Menecrate di Gela, mi dedicò come premio di Zeus.</p>
                <p>Pantares, figlio di Menekrates, proveniente dalla rinomata pianura di Gela, mi dedicò, dopo aver vinto con il cavallo le gare in onore di Zeus.[Alternative translation following Ebert's edition]</p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary" xml:lang="en" resp="#VM">
                <p>The object was interpreted in IvO 142 as a bronze plaque attached to a larger stone base, 
                    but since Jeffery it has generally been identified as the plinth of a small bronze statuette. 
                    One dowel-hole survives, and the plinth was originally attached by two lateral clamps to a larger pedestal, 
                    probably of marble. Because neither the original dimensions of the plinth nor the statuette are preserved, 
                    the longer restorations and the identification of the represented animal remain uncertain.
                The dedicator is generally identified with Pantares, father of the Geloan tyrants Cleandros and Hippokrates 
                    <ref target="https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.01.0125:book=7:chapter=154">(Hdt. 7.154)</ref>, 
                although the identification cannot be established from the inscription alone.</p>
            </div>
